      localeDef: "Locales are estimates from Eurostat's people per km² for each district: urban 2,000+, suburban 500–2,000, mixed town and country 100–500, rural under 100. They aren't the same as the US or Canadian locales.",
      note: "Germany's {rows} districts (Landkreise) and district-free cities (kreisfreie Städte, including Berlin and Hamburg). German schools are run jointly: each state employs the teachers and sets the curriculum, while districts and cities are the school authorities (Schulträger) that build and run the schools, overseen by a school committee (Schulausschuss) of the elected council, the closest thing to a school board. Child poverty is the share of children under 15 receiving basic income support (SGB II) in 2023, from the Bertelsmann Stiftung's Wegweiser Kommune; it leaves out families on other benefits, so it isn't the same as the US, Canadian or other European rates. Meetings are recordings on the district's or city's own YouTube channel: its school committee's sittings where they're posted, otherwise the council's own (Kreistag, Stadtrat, Bürgerschaft) as a stand-in, counted once per day and found for {withMeetings} districts (the Meetings type column says which). Party, church, youth-council and local-TV channels aren't counted, and most councils stream on their own websites, so 0 often means that. Enrollment is pupils at general schools of all types (public and private) at the start of the 2024/25 school year, from the federal and state statistics offices' Regionaldatenbank.",
